WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.360
A cut stalk of celery can go limp and bendy.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.020
Put it in water, and it can turn crisp again.

3
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.720
You drink water. A glass of water does not stiffen your body.

4
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.780
A plant has no bones.

5
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.020
So how does a plant stand up?

6
00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:20.360
And how are its cells different from yours?

7
00:00:21.383 --> 00:00:24.143
Plant cells and animal cells share many parts.

8
00:00:25.023 --> 00:00:28.463
A small part that has its own special jobs is called an organelle.

9
00:00:29.183 --> 00:00:32.203
The nucleus holds the cell's DNA, its instructions.

10
00:00:33.163 --> 00:00:35.283
The cell membrane is a thin outer layer.

11
00:00:36.303 --> 00:00:41.183
Mitochondria release energy from sugar, called glucose, in a form the cell can use.

12
00:00:41.943 --> 00:00:44.163
The cytoplasm is the jelly-like part.

13
00:00:44.983 --> 00:00:48.683
The endoplasmic reticulum is a folded network of membranes.

14
00:00:48.683 --> 00:00:51.523
A vacuole is a sac that stores water.

15
00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:54.427
Plant cells add three more parts.

16
00:00:55.327 --> 00:00:56.687
The first is the cell wall.

17
00:00:57.487 --> 00:00:59.787
It is a stiff layer outside the cell membrane.

18
00:01:00.687 --> 00:01:02.307
It supports and protects the cell.

19
00:01:03.447 --> 00:01:04.727
Animal cells have no cell wall.

20
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.720
The second part is the chloroplast.

21
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.060
Chloroplasts capture radiant energy and make sugar, called glucose.

22
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.480
You find them in green cells.

23
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.160
Plant cells still have mitochondria.

24
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.900
So they can release energy from that sugar, day and night.

25
00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:24.493
The third part is the large central vacuole.

26
00:01:25.213 --> 00:01:28.393
It is one big sac that fills most of a mature plant cell.

27
00:01:29.173 --> 00:01:33.493
It stores water. When water moves into the cell, the vacuole fills.

28
00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:36.393
A full vacuole presses out on the wall.

29
00:01:37.213 --> 00:01:39.153
The wall is stiff, so it pushes back.

30
00:01:39.993 --> 00:01:40.993
The cell gets firm.

31
00:01:41.833 --> 00:01:44.433
Many firm cells side by side make a firm stalk.

32
00:01:45.253 --> 00:01:48.833
When cells lose water, the vacuole shrinks, and the stalk goes limp.

33
00:01:48.833 --> 00:01:49.783
. . .

34
00:01:49.783 --> 00:01:52.223
So how does a plant stand up without a skeleton?

35
00:01:53.243 --> 00:01:57.343
Its cells have stiff walls, and in soft parts, full vacuoles press on them.

36
00:01:58.203 --> 00:01:59.343
Your cells have no walls.

37
00:02:00.323 --> 00:02:02.463
Water inside them does not hold you up.

38
00:02:03.263 --> 00:02:04.603
Your skeleton of bones does.

39
00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:09.583
Plant cells add a cell wall, chloroplasts, and a large central vacuole.
